  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Unaccompanied part-songs for men's voices sung in London gentlemen's clubs and taverns. The catch, a comic and often bawdy round, flourished from the Restoration, when Henry Purcell wrote dozens, and the glee, a short harmonised song for three or more solo voices, flourished from the founding of the Noblemen and Gentlemen's Catch Club in 1761 into the nineteenth century, with Samuel Webbe among its leading composers; the Glee Club and many provincial glee clubs followed.",
